</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Analysis</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Data Exposure Hypothesis: A Test on Post-Cutoff Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary speculation is that the superior in-the-wild performance of modern VFMs is a direct result of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exposure to contemporary synthetic media during pre-training</span>, rather than an intrinsic leap in generalization. To rigorously test this, we constructed a new dataset, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WebAIG-25</span>, designed to serve as a decisive blind test. We built it by collecting synthetic samples posted on the subreddit ‘r/midjourney‘ and real-world photographs from Unsplash. Crucially, all samples were uploaded by users between August 1, 2025, and September 10, 2025. This specific timeframe places the data <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> the pre-training data cutoff for most models under evaluation, making WebAIG-25 a powerful tool to disentangle true generalization from learned artifacts.</p>
</div>
