<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison results on WebAIG-25.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results on this ‘post-cutoff’ dataset, presented in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.1 Data Exposure Hypothesis: A Test on Post-Cutoff Data ‣ 3 Analysis ‣ Brought a Gun to a Knife fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, are revealing and offer strong support for our hypothesis. The most telling evidence comes from the modern VFM families. While the modern <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MetaCLIP-2</span> (77.7%) still outperforms its predecessor <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MetaCLIP</span> (57.7%), its accuracy represents a stark drop from the 91.8% it achieved on Chameleon. A similar, significant drop is also observed for <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Siglip2</span>. This pattern strongly suggests their high performance on older ‘in-the-wild’ sets is less an indicator of true generalization and more a result of prior data exposure. It is this very <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">performance drop</span> on verifiably unseen data that serves as the most compelling evidence, confirming that data exposure—not a fundamental leap in abstract generalization—is the dominant factor driving the remarkable success of modern VFMs on established benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T5" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<div id="S3.T5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:798.2pt;height:219.6pt;vertical-align:-107.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
