<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.T5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of Text–Image Similarities on In-the-Wild Dataset</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Probing VFM’s Latent Alignment with Forgery Concepts</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the source of the superior in-the-wild performance observed in modern foundation models, we speculate that recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) may have learned to associate synthetic images with forgery-related semantic concepts during pre-training. This would provide a powerful, high-level cue for detection that earlier models and non-language-aligned models like DINOv3 might lack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test this hypothesis, we designed a text-image similarity probing experiment exclusively for the VLM-based backbones. Specifically, we randomly sample images from several in-the-wild datasets. For each image, we compute its visual embedding using the VLM’s image encoder and measure its cosine similarity to a set of textual embeddings generated by the corresponding text encoder. These textual concepts are categorized into three groups:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real and Fake-Related Keywords: <em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[‘fake’, ‘real’, ‘AI generated’, ‘authentic’, ‘manipulated’, ‘synthetic’, etc]</em>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Redundant Keywords: <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[‘sunset’, ‘landscape’, ‘portrait’, ‘abstract art’, ‘technology’, ‘nature’, etc]</em>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training Dataset Names: <em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[‘GenImage’, ‘ADM’, ‘BigGAN’, ‘glide’, ‘Midjourney’, etc]</em>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We randomly sampled 2,000 images from each in-the-wild dataset. Then calculated the similarity between these images and our pre-defined text pool, outputting the most textually similar terms for each image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 3.1 Data Exposure Hypothesis: A Test on Post-Cutoff Data ‣ 3 Analysis ‣ Brought a Gun to a Knife fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reveals a significant generational divide in semantic understanding. Older models like <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Siglip</span> consistently fail to associate fake images with forgery-related concepts, instead matching them to generic terms like ‘portrait’ or ‘urban’. Their latent space essentially lacks a concept of "fakeness."</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In stark contrast, modern VLMs like <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MetaCLIP-2</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pe</span> demonstrate a remarkably strong and consistent alignment. Across both established in-the-wild datasets and our new <span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WebAIG-25</span>, their top-matched concepts for fake images are overwhelmingly forgery-related terms like "AI generated" or "midjourney_images". This powerful semantic alignment, learned through exposure to contemporary web data, serves as a decisive shortcut for detection. It confirms that modern VLMs excel not just by recognizing visual artifacts, but by understanding the very concept of a "fake" image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Visualization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The t-SNE visualizations in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Visualization ‣ 3 Analysis ‣ Brought a Gun to a Knife fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> offer a compelling qualitative explanation for the quantitative performance gaps in Tab. <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 2 Evaluation ‣ Brought a Gun to a Knife fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On the curated GenImage dataset</span>, both Meta CLIP-2 and CLIP exhibit similarly entangled feature spaces, which aligns with the comparable, high performance of both our VFM baselines (89.5%) and CLIP-based detectors like <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UnivFD</span> (88.8%). <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">However, this parity vanishes on the in-the-wild Chameleon dataset.</span> There, the performance of <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UnivFD</span> collapses to 57.2%, a failure visually mirrored in its CLIP backbone’s chaotic and inseparable feature space. In stark contrast, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MetaCLIP2</span>’s near-perfect accuracy (91.8%) is directly explained by its t-SNE plot, which reveals a remarkably clean separation between real and fake clusters. This direct link between feature separability and downstream performance confirms that MetaCLIP2’s advantage stems from a fundamentally more discriminative representation for in-the-wild content, acquired through pre-training exposure (Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Visualization ‣ 3 Analysis ‣ Brought a Gun to a Knife fight: Modern VFM Baselines Outgun Specialized Detectors on In-the-Wild AI Image Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12995v1/tsne.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/310;" width="429" height="310" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">T-SNE Visualization of CLIP and Meta CLIP-2 on GenImage and Chameleon.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper demonstrated that for in-the-wild AI-generated image detection, a simple linear classifier built upon a modern Vision Foundation Model (VFM) decisively outperforms specialized forensic detectors, akin to bringing a gun to a knife fight. We attribute this stark performance gap not to architectural novelty but to the VFM’s pre-training on vast, contemporary web data that includes synthetic media. Our findings have two critical implications: first, for practical forensics, leveraging the evolving representations of the latest foundation models is a more robust strategy than designing static detectors. Second, we call for a stricter evaluation protocol where test data is guaranteed to be novel to a model’s entire pre-training corpus to truly assess generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
